---
source: crates/surfaces/cli/src/extension_command/tests.rs
expression: "pinned.join(\"\\n\")"
---
$ specforge x --help
Usage: specforge x <COMMAND>

Commands:
  show   Show one thing
  plain  List plain things

Options:
  -h, --help  Print help

$ specforge x show --help
Show one thing in full: its fields, then its links.

Usage: specforge x show [OPTIONS] <TARGET> <FILE> <SIDE> <COUNT>

Arguments:
  <TARGET>
          The target id

  <FILE>
          

  <SIDE>
          Which side
          
          [possible values: left, right]

  <COUNT>
          How many

Options:
      --label <LABEL>
          A label to show

      --out <OUT>
          

      --status <STATUS>
          Only things with this status
          
          [default: open]
          [possible values: open, closed]

      --limit <LIMIT>
          Return at most this many
          
          [default: 10]

      --shift <SHIFT>
          

      --details
          Show every field

      --strict
          

      --path <PATH>
          A path inside the project (the nearest directory at or above it holding specforge.json; none: the directory itself, with the default configuration)
          
          [default: .]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format
          
          [default: human]
          [possible values: human, json]

      --host <HOST>
          The host calling the command: claude-code, codex or cursor
          
          [possible values: claude-code, codex, cursor]

      --pick <PATH>
          Print only the values at this path of the JSON answer, one per line: `.key`, `[n]`, `[]` (every item), `[kind=<k>]` (the items of that kind), e.g. '.nodes[kind=plan].id'. A string prints bare (a newline in it is printed as is), a number, boolean, object or array as compact JSON, null or a missing value as nothing. It reads the JSON answer: on query, the graph format, with neither --inputs nor --include-coverage

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ specforge x plain --help
List plain things

Usage: specforge x plain [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --path <PATH>      A path inside the project (the nearest directory at or above it holding specforge.json; none: the directory itself, with the default configuration) [default: .]
      --format <FORMAT>  Output format [default: human] [possible values: human, json]
      --host <HOST>      The host calling the command: claude-code, codex or cursor [possible values: claude-code, codex, cursor]
      --pick <PATH>      Print only the values at this path of the JSON answer, one per line: `.key`, `[n]`, `[]` (every item), `[kind=<k>]` (the items of that kind), e.g. '.nodes[kind=plan].id'. A string prints bare (a newline in it is printed as is), a number, boolean, object or array as compact JSON, null or a missing value as nothing. It reads the JSON answer: on query, the graph format, with neither --inputs nor --include-coverage
  -h, --help             Print help
